  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nEarly morning surface analysis places a broad surface ridge directly\nacross eastern Kansas with calm winds and clear skies across much of\nthe area. Temperatures are hovering near freezing and dewpoints in \nthe upper 20s to lower 30s. Given small dewpoint depressions and \ndecoupled boundary layer flow, patchy shallow radiational fog may \ndevelop in low-lying terrain and along the Kansas River valley. Any \nvisibility reductions will rapidly improve after the sun rises.\n\nAloft, a southern Plains shortwave trough continues to shift east-\nsoutheast today, allowing upper-level ridging to amplify over the \ncentral Plains through Tuesday. As the surface ridge slides east of \nthe Missouri River today, light winds will veer to the southeast at \n5 to 10 mph. Mostly sunny skies and weak warm air advection will \nsupport afternoon highs in the mid to upper 50s the area.\n\nLee cyclogenesis deepens across the High Plains Tuesday into\nWednesday ahead of the next Pacific trough entering the western CONUS.\nThe tightening surface pressure gradient will yield breezy southerly\nwinds across the area both Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons, with \nsustained speeds of 10 to 20 mph and gusts of 20 to 30 mph, highest \nacross north-central Kansas toward Concordia and Manhattan. Strong \nsoutherly warm advection will push temperatures above mid-November \nnormals, with highs reaching the upper 50s to near 60 Tuesday and \ninto the 60s on Wednesday.\n\nBy late Wednesday into Wednesday night, the northern Plains surface\nlow will track toward the Upper Midwest, trailing a weak frontal\nboundary across northeast Kansas while subtle shortwave energy ejects\nout of the central Rockies. However, deeper Gulf moisture return\nremains shunted primarily south and east of the local area. Only \nslight chances (15 to 25 percent) for light showers late Thursday \nnight into Friday, with limited QPF expected.\n\nForecast spread increases substantially for Days 5 through 7 (Friday\nthrough Sunday) as a larger-scale upper trough digs across the \nRockies and emerges into the Plains. Ensemble and deterministic \nguidance indicate another surface low developing east of the Rockies \nFriday, bringing a better opportunity for more widespread showers and\npotentially isolated thunderstorms Friday afternoon into Saturday as\nthe associated cold front sweeps through. Because phase speed, \ntrough amplification, and low-level moisture recovery remain \nunresolved at this range, confidence in exact precipitation timing, \nrainfall totals, and post-frontal cooling for next weekend remains \nlow. Dry and cooler conditions are favored to return by Sunday into \nMonday.",
